# A GENERIC-equip `&self` MUTATOR invoked via a NAMED receiver that is a
# bare-value param must leave the CALLER's value untouched — CoW-default-borrow
# means the callee's `Cell[String]` is a private copy.
#
# AXIS CELL — lane = SELF-HOST (with Rust gg as the control). Rust gg has always
# got this right: `method_mutates_receiver` over-approximates to true for any
# `&self`, and `cow_before_mutation` no-ops on owned roots, so it materializes
# and prints Y then A. The self-host mutation-inference pass
# (`compute_method_mutates_self`, self_host_lowerer/lower.gg) classified
# NON-generic equips only (`type_params.len() == 0`), so a generic-equip method
# was absent from `method_mutates_self`, the named-receiver gate answered
# read-only, and the write went straight THROUGH: self-host printed Y then Y.
#
# ⚠ THE KEY IS THE MONO'D NAME. The gate reads
# `type_id_to_name(peel_ptr_tid(rtid)) + "__" + mname`, and both the write and
# the read side go through `mangle_type_name` (`type_to_c_name`'s TNamed arm
# writes it, `map_ast_type` registers `GtNamed(mangled)`, `type_id_to_name` is
# the identity on `GtNamed`). So the classification must be registered under
# `Cell__int64_t__assign`, NOT the bare `Cell__assign` — the generic-instances
# pre-pass is where the mono'd name exists.
#
# RED-VERIFIED on the self-host lane against the pre-fix lowerer: `Y / Y`
# against the intended `Y / A`.
struct Cell[T]:
    T val

equip [T] Cell[T]:
    void assign(&self, T v):
        self.val = v

String touch(Cell[String] c):
    c.assign("Y")
    return c.val

void main():
    Cell[String] orig = Cell[String]("A")
    String inner = touch(orig)
    print(inner)          # Y
    print(orig.val)       # A
